WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.820
So if you've been watching this channel for a little while, you probably know that i am a big fan of cheese

00:00:03.820 --> 00:00:06.140
and I've actually been starting to make my own this year

00:00:06.140 --> 00:00:10.420
I figure if people like beer and they brew they're own beer, and if people like wine and they brew they're own wine

00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:12.100
Why can't I make my own cheese?

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:13.760
Milk is one of those things where it's kind of weird

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:14.800
(Laughs)

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:16.620
it's a really weird byproduct that we eat

00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:18.540
like it's... animal juice

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:20.720
if you ever wonder how cheese is made

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:22.120
this is how its made

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:30.400
(Rhythmic music)

00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:35.220
That white stuff in milk is basically bits of fat proteins and regular proteins.

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:37.080
They are suspended in a liquid.

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:40.660
Usually it's pasteurized, meaning its boiled so there's no pathogens in it.

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:42.900
And the next thing they do to it is they homogenize it

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:45.060
which is basically where they take all those fat particles

00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:47.840
mix it all up so its evenly distributed throughout the milk.

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:51.280
The thing is that breaks down those fat molecules, gok but bad for cheese.

00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:54.280
You want nice, robust, fatty...globs

00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:56.600
in your milk because all those globs -

00:00:56.600 --> 00:00:58.740
you're basically gonna seperate that from your liquid

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:01.140
and those globs impact together and dry out

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:02.580
and that's how you make cheese.

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:06.920
So we wanted to give you guys an update on what's going on with studio four

00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:09.360
because it is coming together really nicely.

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:11.360
So let me take you around and show you what they've done.

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:13.320
Guillermo and the boys have been working hard

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:14.500
starting up here.

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:19.160
They've brought the sheetrock all the way in and buttoned in this window.

00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:23.560
There was a gap in between the original skylight was and where the new skylights fit.

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:25.360
So that's gonna look really nice when it's done

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:28.540
As you can see these beams are in pretty bad shape.

00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:30.720
They need to be sanded, they're super dusty.

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:33.220
So the guys are gonna go through, they're gonna get a rotary sander,

00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:35.440
they're gonna come all the way across here

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:38.240
making this nice and smooth, and then they're gonna make it pop

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:39.420
with some beautiful color.

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:42.040
We got rid of that old, horrible air conditioner

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:45.600
that was about 30 years old and probably had some cancerous materials in it.

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:48.760
It really makes the height of the unit pop out

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:51.820
Guillermo's done a really good job with this renovation so far.

00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:54.020
I think he's got another 2 and a half more weeks to go.

00:01:54.020 --> 00:01:55.520
It's a big day, it's a big thing for us.

00:01:55.520 --> 00:01:57.880
I've dreamed about days with insulation and air conditioning.

00:01:57.880 --> 00:02:02.320
What I'm going to do today is I'm going to take this big gallon of milk and make brie.

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:04.880
Now the process is basically the same for other types of cheese.

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:07.240
Today I have raw milk.

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:11.440
This milk has not been pasteurized, it has not been homogenized.

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:13.400
It is straight up raw milk from the cow.

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:14.400
It's actually kind of hard to find.

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:16.380
It cost me 17 dollars and 50 cents

00:02:16.420 --> 00:02:19.540
but it tastes creamier and it's more rich, more robust

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:24.580
and if I'm gonna sit around and spend hours making my own cheese, why not go the extra mile, right?

00:02:24.580 --> 00:02:27.760
First things first, Carmichael you wanna see what raw milk tastes like?

00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:29.560
fat stuck to the top of it

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:31.860
If I shook this all up you'd start getting butter.

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:34.080
Taste it.

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:45.300
So good.

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:50.440
I grew up, having german milk with my grandparents.

00:02:50.540 --> 00:02:54.980
It's like so close the texture and the taste

00:02:57.040 --> 00:02:58.560
of my German childhood.

00:02:58.720 --> 00:02:59.520
(laughing)

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:00.640
The things you need to make cheese:

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:02.000
2 buckets of water, or bowls

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:03.920
regular water for rinsing stuff off

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:06.020
water with Iodine to keep it sterile

00:03:06.020 --> 00:03:07.540
the pot that I washed off

00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:09.140
I'm going to simply put the milk in.

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:11.100
Now you have to bring the milk up to a specific temperature.

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:13.720
In this case, 90 degrees Fahrenheit (32 degrees celsius)

00:03:13.720 --> 00:03:16.300
That temperature activates the bacteria

00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:18.520
and the mold and promotes growth.

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:22.760
Alright, we're gonna leave that, and let the milk warm up.

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:26.020
Our milk is at the right temperature

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:29.520
and we're gonna put in the bacteria culture.

00:03:29.780 --> 00:03:31.200
Pure bacteria.

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:32.440
Aromatic mesophilic -

00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:34.800
and you can buy this stuff a bunch of different places online.

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:36.800
Different cheeses use different bacterias.

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:39.680
Now, put in the penicillium camemberti,

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:42.580
and this is a mold culture.

00:03:42.580 --> 00:03:46.000
And this is what's going to grow that white mold.

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:46.940
Stir it in.

00:03:46.940 --> 00:03:48.940
Nice and evenly distributed through the milk.

00:03:48.940 --> 00:03:51.400
Let it now sit for 15 minutes.

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:54.940
There's a lot of new players in the market for subscription digital services,

00:03:54.940 --> 00:03:56.940
and we've gotten a few offers to pitch things

00:03:57.300 --> 00:03:59.300
So I'm gonna take you guys through that process of

00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:02.560
what's required when you're pitching a new show to a new client.

00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:06.220
(Drum music)

00:04:06.820 --> 00:04:10.360
Every great idea can be boiled down to two things.

00:04:10.360 --> 00:04:13.380
A great name, and a one-sentence description of everything you need to know.

00:04:13.380 --> 00:04:16.040
I came up with this idea called "Country Ops"

00:04:16.040 --> 00:04:19.800
What does that mean? Well, it's a show about Corridor teaming up with the

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:22.100
most experienced special operations veterans in the world to test

00:04:22.100 --> 00:04:25.020
their field skills in an all-for-fun "redneck" type game scenario.

00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:28.640
This simple description and title is what we would call an "elevator pitch".

00:04:28.640 --> 00:04:30.640
It's called an elevator pitch because you've gotta be able to

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:33.280
tell it to somebody that you don't know in an elevator.

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:34.440
If they don't understand what you're talking about...

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:35.620
you blew it, kid.

00:04:35.620 --> 00:04:37.920
After you go through your description, you want to tell the reader

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:40.160
a little bit more about what they're getting themselves into.

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:43.600
On my scenarios page, I list out game type scenarios

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:46.620
that give a little bit more context to what each episode would be like.

00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:47.460
Demolition derbies

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:48.720
ATV football

00:04:48.760 --> 00:04:49.460
Lawnmower racing

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:50.300
What's not to love?

00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:52.920
Even more important than what you write in a pitch document

00:04:52.920 --> 00:04:54.700
is what you show the viewer.

00:04:54.700 --> 00:04:56.700
So you gotta include things in there

00:04:56.700 --> 00:04:58.700
that are simple and easily convey your

00:04:58.700 --> 00:04:59.860
message.

00:04:59.860 --> 00:05:02.000
Next, I move on to my format page, where I convey

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:04.420
how the show is presented to the audience.

00:05:04.460 --> 00:05:06.080
Now, when someone's going out and spending

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:08.520
a bunch of money on a show, they want to make sure that

00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:10.520
eyeballs end up on that project.

00:05:10.520 --> 00:05:12.520
They aren't able to go and talk to everybody and get

00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:14.720
to know everybody, so you gotta show 'em numbers.

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:17.960
So here, for example, I've shown what our audience demographics are,

00:05:17.960 --> 00:05:19.960
what our geographical demographics are,

00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:21.600
what we're known for online,

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:24.520
and what kind of connections we have that can help get this series done.

00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:27.080
At the end of the day, everything you do boils down

00:05:27.080 --> 00:05:28.780
to trust and credibility.

00:05:28.780 --> 00:05:31.760
And so our reach page just helps emphasize that, in fact,

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:34.080
we do know what we're doing, and we've done this before.

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:35.920
Last thing you want to hit them with a little zinger that

00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:37.580
calls back to the title of the show.

00:05:37.580 --> 00:05:40.040
Here, I used our phrase, "See you in country."

00:05:40.280 --> 00:05:42.280
This gives them a memorable way on how they're gonna

00:05:42.280 --> 00:05:43.620
sell your great idea.

00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:48.560
When it's all said and done, don't be afraid, and let your idea fly into the world!

00:05:48.560 --> 00:05:50.560
No! Nooo! My papers!

00:05:51.100 --> 00:05:51.680
No!

00:05:53.800 --> 00:05:55.100
Good cheese takes a long time.

00:05:55.100 --> 00:05:57.580
I wanted to make Gruyère because that's uh...

00:05:57.580 --> 00:05:59.940
the cheese used in fondue, and I really like fondue.

00:05:59.940 --> 00:06:01.260
It's kind of expensive, too.

00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:02.600
So like "I'll just look up how to make it".

00:06:02.600 --> 00:06:04.600
It takes about a year. (laughs)

00:06:05.260 --> 00:06:07.080
So now we're gonna put in the rennet,

00:06:07.080 --> 00:06:09.440
and rennet is an enzyme that's going to cause the

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:12.120
curd to separate from the whey.

00:06:12.120 --> 00:06:15.520
I'm going to take one of these rennet pills, basically, they're just a capsule,

00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:17.080
dissolve it in some water.

00:06:17.080 --> 00:06:19.260
Now the thing is, you can't use tap water for that

00:06:19.260 --> 00:06:21.260
because tap water has chlorine,

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:24.800
and chlorine will kill your bacteria cultures.

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:27.300
It's the same thing with salt. Salt usually has Iodine in it,

00:06:27.340 --> 00:06:29.660
and at the end, we're going to salt the outside of the cheese

00:06:29.660 --> 00:06:31.280
to keep other molds from growing.

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:33.280
But, if you use salt with iodine,

00:06:33.280 --> 00:06:35.280
it can also kill all the bacteria.

00:06:35.940 --> 00:06:36.440
(Tink)

00:06:36.740 --> 00:06:37.240
(Tink)

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:38.020
(Tink)

00:06:38.860 --> 00:06:39.580
Stir.

00:06:40.360 --> 00:06:41.460
Give it a little shake.

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:45.160
And this is going to now start reacting with the milk.

00:06:45.160 --> 00:06:47.500
Like, for example, you take your blood. You have blood cells,

00:06:47.500 --> 00:06:50.160
but your blood cells are suspended in your blood plasma.

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:53.500
So we're effectively separating the blood cells from the blood plasma.

00:06:53.500 --> 00:06:56.120
You take those blood cells and pack them into a little cylinder

00:06:56.880 --> 00:06:58.240
where they turn into cheese. (laughs)

00:06:58.240 --> 00:07:01.300
After just turning it over in the mold for a couple hours

00:07:01.300 --> 00:07:03.300
and letting the liquid drain out,

00:07:03.300 --> 00:07:06.300
take the solidified block of milk fat now.

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:07.640
I'll put salt on the outside,

00:07:07.640 --> 00:07:09.540
and then put it in the fridge, and that's it.

00:07:09.540 --> 00:07:12.020
That's the extent of work. That's all it takes to make cheese.

00:07:12.020 --> 00:07:14.020
You grab your milk, mix new bacteria, you mold

00:07:14.020 --> 00:07:16.020
the rennet to make it solidify,

00:07:16.020 --> 00:07:17.120
and you walk away, more or less.

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:19.120
There's the whole "putting it in the mold" thing, but it's really

00:07:19.120 --> 00:07:21.060
not a big deal. Like, making cheese is

00:07:21.060 --> 00:07:23.620
really, really easy. I was surprised at how easy it was.

00:07:23.620 --> 00:07:25.620
It just takes time.

00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:28.280
Yeah, that's it. I guess we'll have another cheese party in a couple weeks.

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:31.320
(Laughing)

